1
00:00:00,451 --> 00:00:12,883
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wondering if we can speak about life and death, those two phenomenons, and death being the counterpart of life.

2
00:00:13,824 --> 00:00:19,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But first, there's a sutta, and it's called fragile or perishable.

3
00:00:21,492 --> 00:00:27,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I will teach you the fragile and the unfragile, the perishable and the unperishable.

4
00:00:28,879 --> 00:00:32,262
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the fragile and what is the unfragile?

5
00:00:32,743 --> 00:00:35,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Form is the fragile.

6
00:00:35,886 --> 00:00:39,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Its cessation, subsiding, passing away, is the unfragile.

7
00:00:41,364 --> 00:00:47,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feeling is the fragile, perception is the fragile, intentions are fragile, consciousness is fragile.

8
00:00:48,168 --> 00:00:54,112
[SPEAKER_00]: The cessation, subsiding, passing away, is unfragile, unperishable.

9
00:00:54,132 --> 00:01:00,295
[SPEAKER_00]: That fact of the change remains.

10
00:01:01,436 --> 00:01:04,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those things, those particular things change,

11
00:01:06,595 --> 00:01:16,578
[SPEAKER_00]: In various ways, they're subject to change, but one thing that is not up for debate or subject to change is that.

12
00:01:17,979 --> 00:01:18,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

13
00:01:19,139 --> 00:01:19,640
[SPEAKER_00]: They're ending.

14
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:26,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in terms of, like, we have the phenomenon of life,

15
00:01:32,012 --> 00:01:32,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's counterpart.

16
00:01:33,853 --> 00:01:37,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here's life, everything that I'm experiencing now.

17
00:01:38,676 --> 00:01:40,277
[SPEAKER_00]: The all, if I can say that.

18
00:01:40,337 --> 00:01:41,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

19
00:01:42,198 --> 00:01:43,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Myself included.

20
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:44,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

21
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:45,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything.

22
00:01:46,421 --> 00:01:55,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And the background to that is this change, is this death.

23
00:01:55,547 --> 00:02:00,550
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got to understand death as... the background of death is implicit.

24
00:02:03,703 --> 00:02:10,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because it's usually, like, death is perceived to be something at the end of the life.

25
00:02:11,546 --> 00:02:12,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

26
00:02:13,066 --> 00:02:19,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when that time comes, then death will arrive, so to speak.

27
00:02:20,029 --> 00:02:26,371
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, death is already here, through the fact that you were manifested, that you were born.

28
00:02:27,431 --> 00:02:31,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Already implies, as we say often, like, the

29
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:39,483
[SPEAKER_01]: The manifestation of a thing, anything, any phenomenon, big or small, far or near, doesn't matter.

30
00:02:40,143 --> 00:02:47,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Manifestation of a thing is its impermanence, the fact that it's manifested, the fact that it came to be.

31
00:02:49,207 --> 00:02:52,889
[SPEAKER_01]: It's incomprehensible to you to stop that or prevent that.

32
00:02:52,929 --> 00:02:54,050
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's inaccessible.

33
00:02:55,290 --> 00:03:02,734
[SPEAKER_01]: The manifestation of things, the presentation of phenomena, it's... it's just something that's completely outside of your scope.

34
00:03:04,369 --> 00:03:07,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, death, kind of, it's on... obviously, it's on that level.

35
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:21,893
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just in this... in a general sense, if you take a phenomenon of your life, the all that happens while you're alive, well, the implication of that life is that it is... that it has come to be.

36
00:03:22,773 --> 00:03:23,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It's manifested.

37
00:03:25,013 --> 00:03:29,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So, its cessation is implicit in its manifestation.

38
00:03:30,174 --> 00:03:33,255
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, like, those who are

39
00:03:35,628 --> 00:03:38,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Unmindful, they're as if dead already.

40
00:03:39,571 --> 00:03:44,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't have to wait to die in order to be within death.

41
00:03:45,356 --> 00:03:56,845
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is exist within the Mara's domain, or within the domain of that which implies death, that's already as if dead, because death is there through that implication.

42
00:04:02,055 --> 00:04:02,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you see...

43
00:04:02,995 --> 00:04:11,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you see life, you will see its counterpart existing in the background.

44
00:04:12,937 --> 00:04:13,837
[SPEAKER_00]: It's implication.

45
00:04:13,957 --> 00:04:15,278
[SPEAKER_00]: It's implication.

46
00:04:15,418 --> 00:04:19,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot arise without that implication of death, life.

47
00:04:19,999 --> 00:04:20,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it cannot arise.

48
00:04:21,339 --> 00:04:23,839
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you know, the fact that it is manifested.

49
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:28,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it cannot be conceived separate from.

50
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:31,381
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be conceived without that implication.

51
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:34,292
[SPEAKER_01]: In the broadest sense of that... of that word.

52
00:04:35,092 --> 00:04:38,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is... it's like... well, think of it like... like a simile.

53
00:04:38,214 --> 00:04:42,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Say somebody is living within a waiting trap.

54
00:04:44,979 --> 00:04:46,820
[SPEAKER_01]: The big trap is just waiting to crush you.

55
00:04:48,041 --> 00:04:49,602
[SPEAKER_01]: You're completely unaware of the trap.

56
00:04:50,042 --> 00:04:52,944
[SPEAKER_01]: You're completely unaware of the... the thing hanging over your head.

57
00:04:54,085 --> 00:05:01,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have absolutely... well, by being completely unaware that it's a trap, you have absolutely no notion of escape.

58
00:05:02,167 --> 00:05:04,868
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you were to... even if somebody tell you, oh, it's a trap.

59
00:05:05,728 --> 00:05:07,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't even know where to begin.

60
00:05:08,109 --> 00:05:14,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't even recognize it, you don't even know that there is anything to be escaped or how to be escaped.

61
00:05:14,731 --> 00:05:22,394
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much the existence of anyone who is living within the sensual domain with the wrong view.

62
00:05:23,334 --> 00:05:24,155
[SPEAKER_01]: I being Kutujana.

63
00:05:26,495 --> 00:05:28,116
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they are as if dead already.

64
00:05:29,296 --> 00:05:30,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yes, technically,

65
00:05:31,530 --> 00:05:42,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Their life, their birth, is still manifested there, because they're still existing, but within the domain of the inevitable trap, inevitable cessation of that.

66
00:05:42,794 --> 00:05:54,199
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see there and then, while they're in that state, by not having the right view, by not being a sensory strain, you know, like, they don't even know, they don't even have a notion of the escape.

67
00:05:54,699 --> 00:05:57,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, they might as well be dead already.

68
00:06:00,214 --> 00:06:09,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's the other... if you cultivate the context of death, then you cannot delight in life.

69
00:06:10,901 --> 00:06:11,902
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll turn away from life.

70
00:06:12,082 --> 00:06:25,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, as it says in the Sutta, upekkhā will be established, or the counterpart will be established, but meaning the context will be clear, death in the background, implicit.

71
00:06:26,911 --> 00:06:27,892
[SPEAKER_00]: If you cultivate that

72
00:06:28,132 --> 00:06:28,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there you go.

73
00:06:28,772 --> 00:06:38,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, on the basis of the same simile, if you get the right view and you recognize the trap as a trap, you recognize that whatever has the nature of arising, that's why it's impermanent.

74
00:06:38,356 --> 00:06:41,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature to manifest, that is its impermanence.

75
00:06:42,097 --> 00:06:43,678
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize the extent of the trap.

76
00:06:45,839 --> 00:06:46,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you then contemplate

77
00:06:50,313 --> 00:06:53,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything, in every in-breath, in every out-breath, as the suttas would say...

78
00:06:53,655 --> 00:06:54,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It's implicit.

79
00:06:54,475 --> 00:07:01,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, in that context, and we said it before, but, you know, it doesn't hurt to repeat it.

80
00:07:02,399 --> 00:07:11,783
[SPEAKER_01]: The contemplation of death in the suttas that's often talked about, when it says, like, a monk should contemplate within an in-breath, within an out-breath, he shouldn't, like, he should contemplate death.

81
00:07:11,844 --> 00:07:13,764
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean he's doing it on the level.

82
00:07:13,804 --> 00:07:17,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means that his knowledge, his

83
00:07:19,378 --> 00:07:32,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Correct, concurrent, attending to the implicit death of this life, permeates even the most immediate action of breathing.

84
00:07:32,186 --> 00:07:38,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you contemplate death on the level of in-breath and out-breath, not by watching sensations of in-breath and out-breath.

85
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:45,395
[SPEAKER_01]: You contemplate it by developing that background context of the implication of that death so thoroughly,

86
00:07:46,091 --> 00:07:50,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, even breathing will remain... cannot remain unaffected by that context.

87
00:07:51,796 --> 00:07:56,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, any... even the core of life, the sign of life, breath, heartbeat.

88
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the most neutral kind of habitual

89
00:08:03,437 --> 00:08:05,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Action that you generally take for granted.

90
00:08:05,998 --> 00:08:10,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even that will be permeated by the significance of the implication of death.

91
00:08:11,740 --> 00:08:19,022
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do that not just for the sake of, you know, contemplate death so hard to reach that point where you just kind of... boom!

92
00:08:19,342 --> 00:08:22,323
[SPEAKER_01]: You just step outside of it and you're free.

93
00:08:22,343 --> 00:08:29,466
[SPEAKER_01]: You contemplate it so that there is a complete dispassion towards anything regarding life.

94
00:08:30,414 --> 00:08:32,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you step outside of it.

95
00:08:32,215 --> 00:08:33,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you're free.

96
00:08:33,236 --> 00:08:39,719
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is no more passion towards that step... Well, that removes you.

97
00:08:39,859 --> 00:08:44,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically cancels you within that implication of death.

98
00:08:45,442 --> 00:08:47,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you disappear from the trap.

99
00:08:47,944 --> 00:08:48,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it that way.

100
00:08:49,605 --> 00:08:56,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have no issue if a random trap falls if you are not in that trap.

101
00:08:56,789 --> 00:08:58,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not your problem.

102
00:08:58,129 --> 00:08:59,050
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be your problem.

103
00:09:02,619 --> 00:09:06,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's only a problem when you are the one who's going to be trapped or hit by that thing.

104
00:09:12,748 --> 00:09:14,810
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it...

105
00:09:14,830 --> 00:09:19,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, ordinarily, people will... everybody knows, I'm going to die.

106
00:09:21,636 --> 00:09:25,799
[SPEAKER_00]: But that thought stands for something abstract in the future.

107
00:09:26,079 --> 00:09:29,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Stands for the idea of death, which is like a very much, as I said,

108
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:36,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Misconceived notion of something that will happen at the end, whenever that end is, and so on, in the future, with time...

109
00:09:36,984 --> 00:09:40,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's so disconnected from this life.

110
00:09:40,726 --> 00:09:41,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you don't wait for it.

111
00:09:41,947 --> 00:09:49,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't... Well, you know, if you need to wait for the actual dying to contemplate death, it's too late.

112
00:09:49,451 --> 00:09:56,395
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you contemplate it within life, within that which was born, within that birth, within that manifestation.

113
00:10:03,122 --> 00:10:29,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Although people know that, well, they already, when they think it's at the end of my life, that it ends in the future, even if you had to cultivate their thought every day, for example, like some Stoics might have done, not that they did exactly this, but maybe at the end of their day, they might think, well, I might die tomorrow, or, you know, death is coming, and so on.

114
00:10:30,238 --> 00:10:30,338
[SPEAKER_00]: And

115
00:10:31,888 --> 00:10:36,695
[SPEAKER_00]: And they might cultivate that particular thought, abstract thought, about death being in the future.

116
00:10:37,976 --> 00:10:43,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in the sense that we're talking about, but being somewhere else, at some other point.

117
00:10:43,764 --> 00:10:43,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

118
00:10:44,345 --> 00:10:45,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Not connected with... Yeah.

119
00:10:45,467 --> 00:10:46,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Not connected with...

120
00:10:46,248 --> 00:10:46,989
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a different thing.

121
00:10:47,469 --> 00:10:47,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

122
00:10:48,230 --> 00:10:49,332
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't see it.

123
00:10:51,728 --> 00:10:54,569
[SPEAKER_01]: In the heart of their own present existence.

124
00:10:54,949 --> 00:11:04,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person like that, who, if they had to somehow find the effort to do that every day, have those abstract notions of death, still might be a bit more careful.

125
00:11:04,773 --> 00:11:05,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no, sure.

126
00:11:05,873 --> 00:11:09,454
[SPEAKER_01]: It's better than being completely, like, ignorant or like,

127
00:11:12,532 --> 00:11:14,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Deluded in regard to it, of course.

128
00:11:14,554 --> 00:11:27,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's better to not forget that you will die, even in that kind of abstract, secondary, remote sense, that usual kind of idea of death is for people.

129
00:11:27,670 --> 00:11:28,612
[SPEAKER_01]: It's better than nothing.

130
00:11:28,912 --> 00:11:29,232
[SPEAKER_01]: But,

131
00:11:30,042 --> 00:11:44,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when the Buddha was describing the contemplation of death, that leads to, like, the quickest way of dispassion and arahantship, it has to be taken very, very differently, much more acutely, much more precisely, and much more profusely.

132
00:11:46,665 --> 00:11:56,567
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were thinking about death as an abstract thing in the future, coming, and your mind becomes

133
00:11:58,503 --> 00:12:02,727
[SPEAKER_00]: More grateful for the present things of life.

134
00:12:03,788 --> 00:12:11,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind goes into the direction of delighting in life, really appreciating all the moments and all the things that can happen.

135
00:12:12,135 --> 00:12:15,497
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that is a sign that you are not connecting this.

136
00:12:15,517 --> 00:12:16,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Correct.

137
00:12:16,558 --> 00:12:17,099
[SPEAKER_00]: That's very nice.

138
00:12:17,119 --> 00:12:19,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, take the simile of the trap.

139
00:12:21,442 --> 00:12:22,483
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you're contemplating

140
00:12:25,254 --> 00:12:26,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much any contemplation... Life.

141
00:12:26,676 --> 00:12:26,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

142
00:12:27,077 --> 00:12:29,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Life, if it's done acutely.

143
00:12:29,481 --> 00:12:32,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Death, if it's done acutely.

144
00:12:33,427 --> 00:12:33,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Trap.

145
00:12:34,008 --> 00:12:39,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplation of a trap, acutely, you recognize that, oh, it's not something that will come later.

146
00:12:41,298 --> 00:12:43,761
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there now, as I think it.

147
00:12:44,022 --> 00:12:45,403
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hanging over my head.

148
00:12:46,525 --> 00:12:53,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you then spend time going around saying thank you to other people within the same trap and expressing your gratitude?

149
00:12:53,494 --> 00:12:58,040
[SPEAKER_01]: You might say it in passing, if it comes up, while you are being preoccupied.

150
00:12:58,808 --> 00:13:03,750
[SPEAKER_01]: with how to free myself from the impending thing that's going to crash on my head in this trap.

151
00:13:04,931 --> 00:13:06,532
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm so grateful for this bait.

152
00:13:07,192 --> 00:13:20,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, like, so it's even when people contemplate it, in that sense you described those people, not everybody, but then that death is still kind of removed.

153
00:13:21,619 --> 00:13:24,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, yes, it's a trap and everything, but the fact that you think you have time

154
00:13:27,719 --> 00:13:34,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you go out and appreciate things, you wouldn't appreciate stuff if you're aware of the imminent danger of that stuff.

155
00:13:36,562 --> 00:13:42,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So, only when that imminence is removed, you can kind of, okay, let me deal with this, and then I'll deal with it when it comes.

156
00:13:42,464 --> 00:13:46,325
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like recognizing the imminence, the implicit aspects.

157
00:13:46,445 --> 00:13:47,746
[SPEAKER_00]: The life is the trap.

158
00:13:48,066 --> 00:13:49,206
[SPEAKER_00]: The life is the death.

159
00:13:49,466 --> 00:13:50,587
[SPEAKER_00]: The death, you know.

160
00:13:50,607 --> 00:13:52,267
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot have it otherwise.

161
00:13:52,307 --> 00:13:52,887
[SPEAKER_00]: No, impossible.

162
00:13:53,187 --> 00:13:57,189
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like the separation, because people see it separately.

163
00:13:57,829 --> 00:14:01,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, the separation, because of the the accumulated wrong views.

164
00:14:01,293 --> 00:14:07,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they don't see it simultaneously, as the counterpart to life is, is surrounded.

165
00:14:07,179 --> 00:14:08,760
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, it's, it's shell.

166
00:14:09,761 --> 00:14:20,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And they don't see it simultaneously, because seeing things simultaneously requires training against the grain, against the directionality of your senses, of your mind, of everything you know.

167
00:14:21,735 --> 00:14:23,637
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't do it accidentally.

168
00:14:23,657 --> 00:14:31,503
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't accidentally stumble upon the clear image and the relationship between life and death that would result in the immediate dispassion you become an arahant.

169
00:14:32,123 --> 00:14:46,395
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can, through repetition and through kind of digging up that way, against the grain of your senses, against the grain of life, against that flow of, you know, forward movement, so to speak, that generally is how people's lives go.

170
00:14:47,716 --> 00:14:49,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it takes that effort to swim against the current.

171
00:14:55,330 --> 00:15:15,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And one of the obvious ways of doing that is taking on the precepts, celibacy, sense restraint, and then the contemplation of that, like, death, however remote, delayed, non-imminent might have been, it's gonna start narrowing down.

172
00:15:16,734 --> 00:15:23,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are not maintaining the cover-up of the imminence of death,

173
00:15:24,838 --> 00:15:28,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Through your actions of sensuality, you will be non-restrained.

174
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:29,940
[SPEAKER_00]: You're containing it.

175
00:15:29,980 --> 00:15:31,360
[SPEAKER_00]: You're containing it.

176
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:33,461
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you're going to start discerning it on the right level.

177
00:15:36,502 --> 00:15:41,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those who are mindful of death... Well, that's it.

178
00:15:42,004 --> 00:15:47,306
[SPEAKER_01]: See, not being forgetful of that context is the beginning of that right mindfulness.

179
00:15:47,326 --> 00:15:47,966
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

180
00:15:47,986 --> 00:15:49,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Those who are forgetful, i.e.

181
00:15:49,907 --> 00:15:52,788
[SPEAKER_01]: those who are unmindful, they're as if dead already.

182
00:15:59,337 --> 00:16:07,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And only those who are mindful have an opportunity, have the... To, yeah, eventually penetrate it on that acute level and understand and see it.

183
00:16:08,299 --> 00:16:18,141
[SPEAKER_01]: That would then result, not as I said, in some magical mystical experience, but in complete dispassion towards anything concerned with life.

184
00:16:19,561 --> 00:16:21,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean they delight in death either.

185
00:16:22,882 --> 00:16:28,703
[SPEAKER_01]: They just mean they contemplated sufficiently enough to cancel out delight in life.

186
00:16:31,388 --> 00:16:36,190
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's enough for liberation.

187
00:16:36,210 --> 00:16:39,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, someone gets that context of death.

188
00:16:41,431 --> 00:16:43,292
[SPEAKER_00]: How do they remember it?

189
00:16:43,312 --> 00:16:45,653
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you keep it going throughout the day?

190
00:16:45,873 --> 00:16:47,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you're going to be forgetting it.

191
00:16:47,374 --> 00:16:48,954
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be getting distracted.

192
00:16:49,874 --> 00:16:58,697
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep yourself from drifting away too far from it by policing and restraining your own actions.

193
00:16:59,258 --> 00:17:00,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Which are delighting.

194
00:17:00,573 --> 00:17:03,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Which would be, exactly, which would be countering that context of death.

195
00:17:04,037 --> 00:17:08,201
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you're acting out of sensuality, you have to put life first.

196
00:17:08,422 --> 00:17:11,645
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to put the pleasure of life even more first.

197
00:17:13,247 --> 00:17:16,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a serious perversion of that actual order.

198
00:17:17,832 --> 00:17:20,695
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever you pervert the order, you cover up what's implicit.

199
00:17:22,176 --> 00:17:23,216
[SPEAKER_01]: In the right... in that order.

200
00:17:24,257 --> 00:17:42,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by delighting in life, by perpetuating, proliferating delight through acts of sensuality, acting out of ill-will, acting out of hindrances regarding life and things that come up within that life, you are covering up the implication of death in it.

201
00:17:43,905 --> 00:17:50,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And every time you cover it up, the view that death is something later, gets established more and more.

202
00:17:54,883 --> 00:17:55,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And you...

203
00:17:56,364 --> 00:17:59,126
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose you also have to feel, like, threat.

204
00:18:00,707 --> 00:18:00,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

205
00:18:01,667 --> 00:18:01,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

206
00:18:02,308 --> 00:18:03,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's an immediate threat.

207
00:18:03,949 --> 00:18:05,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... You certainly would, initially.

208
00:18:06,130 --> 00:18:17,117
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's very important, if you want to go down the... down this route of contemplating death, in similar contexts, you better have your aggregator virtue properly established.

209
00:18:18,442 --> 00:18:21,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise the mind is just going to start freaking out in regard to everything.

210
00:18:21,924 --> 00:18:27,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it will realize that there is no shelter up, down, above, below, behind, forward.

211
00:18:27,947 --> 00:18:32,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything, in any direction, I go to seek shelter for myself, it's under death.

212
00:18:33,011 --> 00:18:34,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It's under the rule of death.

213
00:18:34,272 --> 00:18:35,553
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in death, it is implicit.

214
00:18:36,073 --> 00:18:36,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not safe.

215
00:18:38,194 --> 00:18:48,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, only person, they will not be overwhelmed by that, is the person who already contained the mind from spilling over too far and in these irrational attempts of seeking, seeking safety, that would then result in its own derangement.

216
00:18:50,780 --> 00:18:52,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, in a way...

217
00:18:52,121 --> 00:18:58,945
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your aggregate of virtue is developed, then it's... even though death is that threat to life...

218
00:18:58,965 --> 00:19:04,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It can feel as a threat in everybody, but it will be contained, because you have contained that which is under the threat of death.

219
00:19:06,849 --> 00:19:08,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although you still have

220
00:19:09,895 --> 00:19:23,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Some horses in that race, so to speak, in other words, you're not completely, fully dispassionate towards life, you have con... you have contained all those unwholesome ways of making your dependence on that trap even worse.

221
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:36,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you start recognizing the trap and that which would be destroyed by the trap, you might feel some intensity, but overall it's contained because your actions have been purified beforehand.

222
00:19:38,728 --> 00:19:39,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can take it further.

223
00:19:40,829 --> 00:19:57,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person would feel the threat and freak out, only if they haven't contained that... Yeah, they might, like, again, whatever that intensity, at the time, might feel like the highest intensity, because it's not like you can be outside of that experience and compare it.

224
00:19:57,315 --> 00:20:00,937
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like, whatever is the current pain, it's the worst pain in everyone's life.

225
00:20:01,137 --> 00:20:03,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever is the current worry, it's the worst worry.

226
00:20:05,085 --> 00:20:14,127
[SPEAKER_01]: But, whether they're doing it rightly or not, they can see how close to breaking the precept and acting out they are, or not.

227
00:20:15,167 --> 00:20:27,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, in hindsight, when they look back, a week ago, when I started contemplating death and so on, yes, it felt close, it felt acute, it felt scary, but I haven't done anything on account of it.

228
00:20:28,770 --> 00:20:30,190
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will already bring confidence.

229
00:20:31,872 --> 00:20:32,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the measure.

230
00:20:33,173 --> 00:20:34,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you done anything on account of it?

231
00:20:35,054 --> 00:20:36,075
[SPEAKER_01]: And how bad was it?

232
00:20:36,535 --> 00:20:38,356
[SPEAKER_01]: How bad did your mind make you act?

233
00:20:38,556 --> 00:20:39,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Good, yeah.

234
00:20:39,237 --> 00:20:50,164
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the face of that felt intent, that threat, because like, well, I can think about death, I'm not afraid of death, like many people.

235
00:20:50,204 --> 00:20:51,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

236
00:20:51,124 --> 00:20:52,865
[SPEAKER_00]: When I think about death, I'm not afraid of death.

237
00:20:53,206 --> 00:20:54,867
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the death they're thinking about.

238
00:20:57,447 --> 00:21:02,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Gurudev, when you feel a threat of pain, a little bit, what do you do?

239
00:21:04,070 --> 00:21:05,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, there, sure, sure.

240
00:21:05,850 --> 00:21:24,577
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like if a person wants to contemplate, start contemplating death, if they say, feel like their virtue is decent, they've been restraining, guarding the sense doors, they're not breaking the precepts, they're not engaging with sensuality, they're celibate and so on, and they want to practice, you know, a bit of a

241
00:21:26,820 --> 00:21:33,786
[SPEAKER_01]: A sharper approach, such as death contemplation, you don't start by your ideas of death.

242
00:21:34,087 --> 00:21:38,050
[SPEAKER_01]: You just pick up something that's dear to you and imagine it being taken away.

243
00:21:39,111 --> 00:21:42,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Where that feeling of pain is, that's where death is.

244
00:21:42,694 --> 00:21:46,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take it and further there and practice dispassion towards it.

245
00:21:47,836 --> 00:21:57,199
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also, yes, finding that those signs of death, almost, you know, picking up that flavor of death, you know, seeing, imagining...

246
00:21:57,279 --> 00:21:58,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

247
00:21:58,299 --> 00:22:08,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those flavors are actually to be found in things that you don't... that somebody might not necessarily associate with death, with their idea of death.

248
00:22:09,582 --> 00:22:11,163
[SPEAKER_01]: They're finally not getting what you want.

249
00:22:14,597 --> 00:22:16,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Being paired with what you don't want.

250
00:22:16,498 --> 00:22:18,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Being separated from what you want.

251
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:18,660
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things.

252
00:22:18,700 --> 00:22:19,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, dukkha.

253
00:22:19,140 --> 00:22:20,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are examples of dukkha.

254
00:22:20,742 --> 00:22:24,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Wherever there is dukkha, the direction of dukkha, that's where death is.

255
00:22:25,805 --> 00:22:28,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contemplate things that would cause you suffering.

256
00:22:31,910 --> 00:22:33,211
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are contemplating death.

257
00:22:33,591 --> 00:22:36,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you take it further and it's like, yeah, so I would be afraid.

258
00:22:36,594 --> 00:22:37,394
[SPEAKER_01]: I would be...

259
00:22:37,434 --> 00:22:38,175
[SPEAKER_01]: I would be affected.

260
00:22:38,215 --> 00:22:40,757
[SPEAKER_01]: I would suffer if I lose the loved ones.

261
00:22:42,338 --> 00:22:43,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, imagine how much more then.

262
00:22:44,671 --> 00:22:53,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through that same dukkha of contemplating losing the loved ones, imagine losing also these senses on account of which you have your loved ones.

263
00:22:54,433 --> 00:23:00,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's gonna feel even worse.

264
00:23:00,855 --> 00:23:06,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so have... imagine those things, contemplate that, but then pick up this... that... the sign of death, basically.

265
00:23:06,497 --> 00:23:07,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, feel it.

266
00:23:07,457 --> 00:23:07,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Feel it.

267
00:23:07,917 --> 00:23:08,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Feel it.

268
00:23:10,554 --> 00:23:18,800
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, as I said, only on the basis of thoroughly developed virtue, because otherwise...

269
00:23:20,261 --> 00:23:23,604
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, this is not something that the Buddha was teaching to householders.

270
00:23:24,164 --> 00:23:26,326
[SPEAKER_01]: By householders, meaning people that are not celibate.

271
00:23:27,346 --> 00:23:33,491
[SPEAKER_01]: People that might occasionally take on virtue and, you know, go to monastery and keep their priesthood for a week or something.

272
00:23:35,157 --> 00:23:47,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for most people who love life, love things in life, their families, their children, whatever, getting the flavor of death,

273
00:23:48,830 --> 00:23:54,136
[SPEAKER_00]: It means turning away from it and forgetting about it, never thinking about it again, and just indulging more into life.

274
00:23:54,276 --> 00:23:58,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Love life even harder, because just cover it up.

275
00:23:58,760 --> 00:24:06,889
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you had to give such a person the threat of death, while they were still delighting so much in that life... Well, you get what the suttas describe.

276
00:24:07,410 --> 00:24:09,151
[SPEAKER_01]: When they heard the Buddha describe nibbāna,

277
00:24:09,872 --> 00:24:21,179
[SPEAKER_01]: From their standpoint of very much cherishing life, possessions, and everything else, they would have, as the suttas describe, they fell on the ground, beating their breasts, pulling their hair, and saying like, oh, that will kill us, that will destroy us.

278
00:24:21,400 --> 00:24:21,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

279
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:31,586
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like people in, on death row, in some of these, in America, they don't, they're on death row, but they, they could be executed any day.

280
00:24:32,287 --> 00:24:33,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's always this threat.

281
00:24:33,948 --> 00:24:35,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Hanging over their head, yeah.

282
00:24:35,309 --> 00:24:37,610
[SPEAKER_00]: And they are not really wanting to be there.

283
00:24:37,830 --> 00:24:38,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

284
00:24:39,398 --> 00:24:43,840
[SPEAKER_00]: They would rather not distract themselves from that, which they go crazy, you know.

285
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:44,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

286
00:24:44,380 --> 00:24:46,141
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the worst kind of torture.

287
00:24:46,161 --> 00:24:50,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, that's what Dostoevsky was writing about in The House of Dead.

288
00:24:51,503 --> 00:24:58,545
[SPEAKER_01]: In the prison, from his experience in the prison, basically, he saw that the worst punishment to give to a man that, you know, a man, i.e.

289
00:24:58,845 --> 00:25:07,769
[SPEAKER_01]: a householder that loves life, that does not see the problem in sensuality, is to, like, have a perpetual possibility of execution.

290
00:25:08,662 --> 00:25:13,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there were people there, they were insane, in their death row, and they might be killed tomorrow, they might not.

291
00:25:13,805 --> 00:25:17,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they were taken out to the gallows, and then sent back.

292
00:25:17,967 --> 00:25:20,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like unbearable, mentally.

293
00:25:20,648 --> 00:25:22,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And they all just disintegrated.

294
00:25:22,109 --> 00:25:24,610
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was, he was affected by, by witnessing that.

295
00:25:25,410 --> 00:25:28,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as that other sutta describes, it's like, eh...

296
00:25:29,044 --> 00:25:37,029
[SPEAKER_01]: When the man was talking to the Buddha, you brought it up recently, when he said, oh, you know, like, we love life, family, centrality, household life.

297
00:25:37,749 --> 00:25:42,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Just the idea of renunciation feels like a precipice, feels like falling into the abyss.

298
00:25:43,357 --> 00:25:45,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Just the idea of renunciation.

299
00:25:45,399 --> 00:25:52,224
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, if renunciation scares you that much, well, imagine how much forceful renunciation will scare you.

300
00:25:52,585 --> 00:25:53,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Death.

301
00:25:53,165 --> 00:25:53,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

302
00:25:53,706 --> 00:25:56,548
[SPEAKER_01]: When things are forcefully taken away from you.

303
00:25:57,149 --> 00:26:04,355
[SPEAKER_01]: When you are... basically, where your own mind does the renunciation for you, like, without you wanting it.

304
00:26:05,556 --> 00:26:06,797
[SPEAKER_01]: When your senses just fail.

305
00:26:08,079 --> 00:26:12,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, you already get the flavor through practicing renunciation.

306
00:26:12,501 --> 00:26:14,261
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's just generally so scary.

307
00:26:14,281 --> 00:26:16,422
[SPEAKER_01]: You say to people, you gotta be celibate.

308
00:26:16,462 --> 00:26:19,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, like, you put on the greatest fear on them.

309
00:26:20,763 --> 00:26:21,744
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, forever?

310
00:26:21,844 --> 00:26:24,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I will never experience the pleasure of the senses again?

311
00:26:24,665 --> 00:26:25,725
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, it's like death.

312
00:26:27,686 --> 00:26:37,289
[SPEAKER_01]: From the point of view of those whose minds are still very much imbued with sensuality and do not see danger in that trap.

313
00:26:39,973 --> 00:26:55,898
[SPEAKER_01]: But seeing it as danger, no longer delighting in life, which... Yeah, so, like, you brought up the example of a person who, say, might experience that flavor and then double down in commitment to the life and loving life and family and so on.

314
00:26:56,479 --> 00:26:57,279
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like...

315
00:26:57,299 --> 00:27:09,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody experiences the flavor of death, gets a hint of it, and they might... they recognize they're not able to, like, dwell on it and contemplate it rightly,

316
00:27:10,627 --> 00:27:13,468
[SPEAKER_01]: At least, don't cover it up twice as much as before.

317
00:27:14,589 --> 00:27:20,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, allow that kind of background hint of that doubt, that fear, to stay there.

318
00:27:21,512 --> 00:27:27,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, you don't have to go fully towards it, if you have not prepared the basis of virtue, sense of strength, liberty and so on.

319
00:27:28,556 --> 00:27:30,056
[SPEAKER_01]: But, at least, don't cover it up.

320
00:27:31,971 --> 00:27:33,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't completely cover up.

321
00:27:33,132 --> 00:27:33,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were.

322
00:27:33,852 --> 00:27:34,633
[SPEAKER_01]: They were puthujjana.

323
00:27:34,653 --> 00:27:35,594
[SPEAKER_01]: They were philosophers.

324
00:27:35,614 --> 00:27:36,654
[SPEAKER_01]: They were religious people.

325
00:27:36,875 --> 00:27:37,955
[SPEAKER_00]: They weren't so intoxicated.

326
00:27:37,975 --> 00:27:38,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

327
00:27:39,176 --> 00:27:41,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And they were not withdrawn from sensuality and so on.

328
00:27:41,458 --> 00:27:47,662
[SPEAKER_01]: But they did have the capacity, through practice, to not completely cover that up.

329
00:27:47,902 --> 00:27:50,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And it did shape their life more wholesomely.

330
00:27:51,525 --> 00:27:53,887
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not ideal, because they still stay puthujjanas.

331
00:27:54,810 --> 00:28:07,793
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm saying is, if somebody who also has access to the instruction of the dhamma, and then practices that non-complete cover-up of death, well, it would certainly bring you even closer to the right view.

332
00:28:08,413 --> 00:28:16,275
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, you realize, you might realize, that giving up sensuality is not as scary as you thought it was.

333
00:28:17,515 --> 00:28:24,037
[SPEAKER_01]: And you work up towards it, and then... and actually start containing your mind on that level where it matters.

334
00:28:26,288 --> 00:28:35,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you do see it right, death rightly, and you start renouncing sensuality, start renouncing the world... Well, no, you gotta start...

335
00:28:36,351 --> 00:28:38,811
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, you might have that, you know, in the beginning...

336
00:28:38,831 --> 00:28:40,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, don't cover it up and then start renouncing.

337
00:28:40,932 --> 00:28:41,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure, sure, sure.

338
00:28:41,912 --> 00:28:43,373
[SPEAKER_01]: That can be like a wake-up call.

339
00:28:44,033 --> 00:28:44,693
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm going to die.

340
00:28:45,353 --> 00:28:45,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

341
00:28:46,433 --> 00:28:49,094
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's no way I can escape that, so to speak.

342
00:28:50,415 --> 00:28:55,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you escape the implication of manifestation?

343
00:28:56,610 --> 00:29:01,691
[SPEAKER_01]: When you yourself, anything you know about yourself, any possession, any ownership, is just another manifestation.

344
00:29:02,972 --> 00:29:03,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

345
00:29:04,452 --> 00:29:22,457
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person who's actually moved by that thought and starts doing things rightly, in accordance with it, starts to, like, let me stop being a bit careful, let me find an answer to this issue, and then starts to contemplate it more and more.

346
00:29:24,018 --> 00:29:29,823
[SPEAKER_00]: And finds himself, you know, virtuous, dispassionate towards life.

347
00:29:31,444 --> 00:29:37,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, as that sutta says, you know, either he is fully dispassionate towards life, or upādāna gets established.

348
00:29:39,431 --> 00:29:43,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like he's in this paranoid fear.

349
00:29:43,436 --> 00:29:44,677
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

350
00:29:44,717 --> 00:29:46,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Or disgust and hates everything.

351
00:29:46,699 --> 00:29:48,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

352
00:29:49,022 --> 00:29:56,330
[SPEAKER_01]: He might have episodes of that, but again, with the right instruction, which is abundant with the suttas and so on, you recognize, okay, no, it's about

353
00:29:58,102 --> 00:30:09,248
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole dwelling on death, and recollecting it, and not forgetting it, is just so to experience, to develop the complete dispassion towards anything concerned with life.

354
00:30:09,408 --> 00:30:10,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to that extent.

355
00:30:10,689 --> 00:30:11,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Not more, not less.

356
00:30:13,390 --> 00:30:19,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that dispassion is established, you recognize, okay, so now I neither delight in that life, nor do I delight in death.

357
00:30:20,294 --> 00:30:22,635
[SPEAKER_01]: I certainly don't fear it, because I have nothing to lose anymore.

358
00:30:23,884 --> 00:30:29,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever's here, will have to not be here, because it's here, for no other reason.

359
00:30:30,527 --> 00:30:40,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceiving life, seeing it correctly, you will see, well, you're basically contemplating in each of their life, counterpart, death.

360
00:30:41,791 --> 00:30:42,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, of course.

361
00:30:42,451 --> 00:30:46,313
[SPEAKER_00]: You just... and the realization, it's not mine.

362
00:30:47,373 --> 00:30:48,614
[SPEAKER_00]: This is never was mine.

363
00:30:48,634 --> 00:30:49,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

364
00:30:49,275 --> 00:30:52,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Through this passion, because passion maintains the ownership.

365
00:30:52,478 --> 00:30:54,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of passion maintains the ownership.

366
00:30:54,580 --> 00:30:59,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Possession, consumption, all these ideas, all these notions, all these phenomena, it's just how you maintain the ownership.

367
00:30:59,704 --> 00:31:00,205
[SPEAKER_01]: The ownership.

368
00:31:00,465 --> 00:31:06,290
[SPEAKER_01]: When you realize that, when you start contemplating and establish the context that's incompatible,

369
00:31:07,343 --> 00:31:15,907
[SPEAKER_01]: With the growth of passion towards things, the mind just can't go in that direction because of the whole framework of death that's being seen in regard to it.

370
00:31:16,507 --> 00:31:21,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you go and reach this fruit to eat it, easily, you enjoy it.

371
00:31:21,609 --> 00:31:29,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you suddenly recognize, through discernment, that there is a massive axe waiting to chop your hand each time you reach for the fruit.

372
00:31:29,452 --> 00:31:29,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh!

373
00:31:30,873 --> 00:31:34,034
[SPEAKER_01]: The significance, the joy of the fruit very much changes.

374
00:31:34,774 --> 00:31:36,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes actually dangerous.

375
00:31:37,148 --> 00:31:38,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the taste hasn't changed.

376
00:31:39,070 --> 00:31:39,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It all remained the same.

377
00:31:40,010 --> 00:31:40,891
[SPEAKER_01]: It still tastes the same.

378
00:31:40,991 --> 00:31:43,233
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest fruit ever known to man.

379
00:31:44,354 --> 00:31:47,496
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you see an axe waiting to fall.

380
00:31:48,798 --> 00:31:51,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, oh, it's not the same thing anymore, isn't it?

381
00:31:51,740 --> 00:31:55,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind will not go and just, oh, yeah, yeah, give me that fruit, let me reach for it.

382
00:31:56,104 --> 00:31:57,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it sees what's implicit.

383
00:31:59,486 --> 00:32:00,926
[SPEAKER_01]: And you contemplate it long enough.

384
00:32:01,086 --> 00:32:06,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It will stop being scary, because you recognize, well, there's nothing scary about an axe hanging over a random fruit.

385
00:32:07,668 --> 00:32:10,009
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes scary when my hand is in between the two.

386
00:32:11,149 --> 00:32:11,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Or my head.

387
00:32:14,030 --> 00:32:19,391
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, when you recognize the danger, you don't have to... Well, you have to practice sense restraint because of the habits.

388
00:32:20,291 --> 00:32:22,912
[SPEAKER_01]: But, when that's been thoroughly done, you don't have to then

389
00:32:24,289 --> 00:32:25,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop yourself from reaching.

390
00:32:26,150 --> 00:32:30,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Just by having that context, means mind will not be reaching.

391
00:32:30,153 --> 00:32:37,158
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, whatever you frequently think and ponder, that's what will become the inclination of your mind.

392
00:32:37,318 --> 00:32:41,221
[SPEAKER_01]: You're frequently pondering about the taste of the fruit, and how great it is, and the texture.

393
00:32:41,922 --> 00:32:48,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the mind isn't losing its broader context, and it's becoming more obsessed and focused on the aspect of the taste.

394
00:32:48,847 --> 00:32:49,347
[SPEAKER_01]: The beauty.

395
00:32:49,567 --> 00:32:50,808
[SPEAKER_01]: The beauty, exactly.

396
00:32:51,168 --> 00:32:53,330
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you frequently contemplate the hanging axe,

397
00:32:55,124 --> 00:33:01,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then the mind is inclining towards avoiding that danger, because it's factually dangerous.

398
00:33:03,014 --> 00:33:04,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, restraint becomes effortless.

399
00:33:06,097 --> 00:33:09,001
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to restrain yourself, because you don't want to do it in the first place.

